## TODO: kill-pd3-fallback

Independent of the multi-source phases; do before scaling past a handful of games.

### Context

`engine_for_game` maps any unknown game id to `&PD3_ENGINE`; `game_def_for_id`
does the same; nearly every Tauri command starts with
`game_id.as_deref().unwrap_or("pd3")`. With 4 games and disciplined call sites it
is survivable; with many games, a typo'd or missing gameId silently reads/writes
PD3's directories - a data-corruption class of bug.

### What to do

- `engine_for_game` and `game_def_for_id` return Result/Option; unknown game id is
  a hard command error, never a silent PD3.
- Retire the `Option<String> game_id` backward-compat defaults on the IPC surface:
  make gameId required in api.ts wrappers and in the Rust command signatures. The
  renderer always has `activeGame`; the Option-ness only exists for pre-multi-game
  compat.
- Sweep: `unwrap_or("pd3")` across src-tauri/src/commands/ (mods/mod.rs,
  launchers/mod.rs, settings.rs, news.rs).
- Note for the far future, do not act now: games are compile-time Rust statics +
  a TS union (`GameId`, `GAMES`, engine.rs statics, launchers/games/*.rs). Each
  new game is code + a release, and history shows each game needed bespoke
  subsystems anyway (crimeboss_settings.rs, pdmod.rs, host_mods.rs,
  ue4ss_modstxt.rs) - roughly 60% config, 40% reverse-engineering. A data-driven
  game registry only pays off if game count grows past what hand-written modules
  sustain; revisit then, not preemptively.
